# The persistent shell lost every `cd` and `export` made by a command slower than 5 seconds (2026-10-04)

Five different tests in `xai-grok-tools` had been failing one at a time, on no schedule,
only inside the full suite. They are written about different things (an exported variable,
a shell function, a `$()` capture, a deleted working directory, a spawn error) and their
assertions look nothing alike. Instrumenting the one place where shell state changes hands
shows one cause behind all five: the deadline that bounds reading a state dump was armed
when the command was *spawned*, so any command that took longer than it returned "no dump",
and the session kept the state from before that command. Nothing above the reader knew.

== 0. What the suite kept saying

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib

Nine full rounds before any instrumentation, and then five more with it, produced these
five names. Each is quoted with the message the test itself printed.

    test_persistent_shell_env_var_persists
        assertion `left == right` failed: env var should persist across commands
          left: ""
         right: "hello123"

    test_persistent_shell_function_persists
        assertion `left == right` failed
          left: Some(127)
         right: Some(0)

    test_persistent_shell_variable_capture
        assertion `left == right` failed: variable from command substitution should persist
          left: ""
         right: "captured_value"

    test_persistent_shell_deleted_cwd_falls_back_to_request_cwd
        fallback warning must be in the command output, got: "/tmp\n"

    test_persistent_shell_spawn_error_names_missing_cwd
        spawn must fail when both directories are missing

`Some(127)` is a command-not-found, and "spawn must fail" firing means the spawn
succeeded. Every one of them is the same statement in different clothes: the state the
persistent shell was supposed to carry into the next command was not carried. All five
`cd` / `export` / function definitions are made by the *first* command of the test and
read back by the second, and every one of them passed when run alone.

== 1. Instrumenting the hand-over

`ShellState::update_from_dump` returns a bool saying whether it accepted the dump, and its
only caller ignored the bool and logged at `debug`
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs:1543`). A dump that never
arrived, a dump that arrived truncated, and a dump for the wrong shell were therefore
indistinguishable in the logs and indistinguishable in the suite.

The probe prints both arms of that decision, and the arm of the reader that hands it an
empty string, gated on an environment variable so the shipped binary is unaffected:

    SHELLDUMPPROBE accept len=<bytes>
    SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=<bytes> head=<first 40 bytes> tail=<last 40 bytes>
    SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/probe-shell-state.py 5

The driver patches `shell_state.rs`, builds, runs the suite once per requested round, and
restores the file with `shutil.copy2` plus an `os.utime` of the original timestamps,
checking the result with `filecmp.cmp(shallow=False)`. Final line of its own output:

    restored byte-identically: True

Five rounds on a 28-core box, running the suite at full parallelism:

    ==== round 1: rc=101 accept=2 reject=2 timeout=2 ====
        FAILED computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_spawn_error_names_missing_cwd
        FAILED computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_deleted_cwd_falls_back_to_request_cwd
        SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
        SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
        SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout
        SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout
    ==== round 2: rc=101 accept=1 reject=1 timeout=1 ====
        FAILED computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_variable_capture
        SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
        SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout
    ==== round 3: rc=0 accept=0 reject=0 timeout=0 ====
    ==== round 4: rc=101 accept=1 reject=1 timeout=1 ====
        FAILED computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_spawn_error_names_missing_cwd
        SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
        SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout
    ==== round 5: rc=101 accept=1 reject=1 timeout=1 ====
        FAILED computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_deleted_cwd_falls_back_to_request_cwd
        SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
        SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout

libtest captures a test's stderr and echoes it only under the `failures:` heading, so
these counts come from the failing tests' own buffers and are lower bounds on what the
run did. That is what makes them usable: the lines are attributed to the test that
printed them. Round 4, verbatim:

    ---- computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_spawn_error_names_missing_cwd stdout ----
    SHELLDUMPPROBE read_timeout
    SHELLDUMPPROBE reject len=0 head="" tail=""
    SHELLDUMPPROBE accept len=464133

    thread 'computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_spawn_error_names_missing_cwd' panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs:4953:13:
    spawn must fail when both directories are missing

That test runs `cd <tmpdir>` and then a `pwd` whose `working_directory` no longer exists,
and expects the spawn itself to fail. The `read_timeout` is the first command's dump; the
`reject len=0` is the caller receiving the empty string that timeout returns; the `cd` is
therefore never in the state, so the second command spawns in a directory that still
exists, and the assertion below it is unreachable. In every failing round the number of
`read_timeout` lines equals the number of `reject len=0` lines, and the one green round
printed neither.

== 2. Where the clock was armed

The reader is started for a command when that command is spawned
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs:878`):

    let dump_handle =
        tokio::spawn(
            async move { shell_state::read_dump_from_pipe(prep.state_out_read).await },
        );

and the read it runs was wrapped in one deadline
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/shell_state.rs:681`, before the fix):

    match tokio::time::timeout(
        DUMP_READ_TIMEOUT,                       // 5s
        tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || { ...read until EOF or END marker... }),
    )

so the 5 seconds had to cover everything between the fork and the last byte of the dump:
login-environment replay is outside it, but the per-command snapshot is not, the command
itself obviously is not, and the drain of the dump is. The dump is not small on a real
user's shell. The `accept` line above read `len=464133`; that is the state of this host's
zsh, about 454 KB, pushed through a pipe whose buffer is 64 KiB, so the reader has to be
awake and draining for the whole of it.

What tipped it over was load. The suite runs at 32-way parallelism, each test spawning a
shell that replays a snapshot, and the deadline is a wall-clock one. A `sleep`-free test
command like `cd /tmp/xxx` needs well under 5 seconds of CPU; it does not get it reliably
when 32 of them are queued behind each other.

The dump is written by the shell as its last act, so the only moment at which a silent
dump pipe means anything is after the shell is gone. That is where the bound belongs.

== 3. Two hypotheses that died first

- The END marker being split across two writes, so the reader's `buf.contains(END_MARKER)`
  test and the caller's `raw.ends_with("MARKER\n")` test disagree. `strace` on
  `bash -c 'printf "%s\n" M'`, on `bash -c 'echo M'` and on
  `zsh -c 'builtin print -r -- "M"'` shows one `write()` of marker plus newline every
  time. A marker at the end of a large dump is still one write, and pipe writes below
  `PIPE_BUF` are atomic.
- The poll loop reading the output pipes incorrectly. It was not: that question was the
  `test_output_preserved_on_timeout` investigation, whose tick trace is in
  `docs/verification/terminal-timeout-budget-2026-10-04.log`. Different defect, same week.

== 4. The fix

`read_dump_from_pipe` has no clock now: it reads to the END marker or to EOF, which is what
it already did, and returns whatever arrived. The bound moved to the one caller that waits
for it, where the child is already known to have exited
(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/terminal.rs:1523`,
`collect_shell_state_dumps`):

    match tokio::time::timeout(shell_state::DUMP_COLLECT_TIMEOUT, handle).await {

`DUMP_READ_TIMEOUT` became `DUMP_COLLECT_TIMEOUT`, same 5 seconds, documented as a bound on
the wait rather than on the command. The paths that kill a child already `abort()` the
reader (`shutdown_all`, `kill_foreground_commands`, `kill_and_finalize`), so nothing is
left to wait on a pipe that will never be written again except this one bounded wait.

The silence went too. A dump rejected after a clean exit is now a `warn` carrying the byte
count, which is what separates the empty string from a truncated dump:

    tracing::warn!(dump_len = dump.len(), "shell state dump rejected after a clean exit, ...")

and a wait that ran the whole grace is a `warn` on its own. A killed shell never reaches
its dump, so a rejection after a signal stays at `debug`: that case is expected, and
warning on it would fire on every timed-out command.

== 5. What pins it, and what each mutant costs

Six tests cover the hand-over, three on each side of it. Run together on an idle host:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib -- \
          computer::local::shell_state::tests::dump_written_long_after_the_reader_started_is_still_read \
          computer::local::shell_state::tests::waiting_for_the_next_dump_chunk_does_not_park_the_runtime_thread \
          computer::local::shell_state::tests::giving_up_on_the_dump_reader_closes_our_end_of_the_pipe \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_state_survives_a_slow_command \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_wedged_dump_pipe_does_not_stall_the_reply \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_giving_up_on_the_dump_releases_the_pipe

    test result: ok. 6 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3209 filtered out; finished in 9.39s

The first is the reader's own contract: a writer sleeps 6 seconds, longer than the deadline the
reader used to carry, then writes a whole dump, and the reader has to hand it back intact. The
second is the one written for this slice's own defect (section 6). The third is the close (section
7). The fourth is the user-visible symptom on the shipped tool path: a persistent-shell command
that runs `cd`, `export` and `sleep 6` must still leave both behind for the next command, and the
test asserts that its own command outlived the grace, so it cannot quietly decay into a fast
command. The fifth and sixth are the case the removed clock used to be justified by: a
backgrounded command inherits the dump pipe, the shell then kills itself, and the dump never
comes. The fifth asks for the reply on the grace rather than when the grandchild gives up; the
sixth asks that giving up on the wait also gave up on the read.

Each mutant is applied to the working tree, run, and restored byte-identically by
`{SCRATCH}/mutate-dump.py`. All four were re-run on an idle host, because the first set of numbers
was taken on a machine that turns out to have been running 52 spin loops (section 9). The lines
below are that second set.

Mutant m1, put the deadline back inside the reader (`{SCRATCH}/mut-m1-reader-clock.log`):

    test dump_written_long_after_the_reader_started_is_still_read ... FAILED
    panicked at crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/shell_state.rs:1013:81:
    called `Result::unwrap()` on an `Err` value: EPIPE

    test test_persistent_shell_state_survives_a_slow_command ... FAILED
    assertion `left == right` failed: the slow command should have run to completion: ""
      left: None
     right: Some(0)

    test result: FAILED. 4 passed; 2 failed; ... finished in 7.36s

The writer finds out before the assertion does: the reader stopped draining, so the write into the
pipe came back EPIPE. The second failure is the defect in one line, a command killed by its own
deadline before it reached its `cd` and `export`.

Mutant m4, leave the fd blocking (`{SCRATCH}/mut-m4-blocking-fd.log`): see section 6. Mutants m2
and m3, the two ways the wait can be unbounded, are in sections 6 and 7.

== 6. The reader moved onto the reactor, which is where the blocking read showed up

Section 4 left the reader on `spawn_blocking`, and that is not a wait a caller can give up on:
dropping the `JoinHandle` leaves the closure on a pooled worker, parked in `read(2)`, holding both
that worker and our end of the pipe for as long as any process keeps a write end open. Reading the
kill paths showed why nobody noticed: `shutdown_all`, `kill_foreground_commands` and
`kill_and_finalize` all `abort()` the reader, and killing the process group closes the pipe, so
the thread always did end up finishing. The cost was one leaked pooled worker per command that
was killed or timed out *and* had backgrounded something. The read moved to the reactor, where
cancelling the task is what closes the fd:

    set_nonblocking(&fd)?;
    let fd = AsyncFd::new(fd)?;
    loop {
        let mut guard = fd.readable().await?;
        match guard.try_io(|inner| nix::unistd::read(inner.get_ref(), &mut chunk)) { ... }
    }

The first version of that loop was wrong, and the first diagnosis of the wrongness was wrong too.
The symptom was the new actor test taking 61 seconds for a 5 second grace. The diagnosis was that
`AsyncFd` was handing back stale readiness, so the fix seemed to be a `clear_readiness()` call in a
would-block arm. That does not compile: on tokio 1.52.3 the method is `clear_ready()`. Reading the
source then removed the whole idea, because `AsyncFdReadyGuard::try_io` clears readiness itself
when the closure returns a `WouldBlock` error, which also makes an `Ok(Err(WouldBlock))` arm
unreachable.

Unreachable is the answer, not an inconvenience: a `read(2)` on a blocking fd never returns
EAGAIN, it sleeps in the kernel. `os_pipe()` creates the pipe with `pipe2(O_CLOEXEC)`, which says
nothing about `O_NONBLOCK`, so `AsyncFd` was only given the *waiting*; the read was still that one
system call, and the kernel took the single thread of a current-thread runtime with it. Every
timer in that runtime stopped, which is the 61 seconds, and `arm:tick` vanished from the trace for
exactly that reason.

`waiting_for_the_next_dump_chunk_does_not_park_the_runtime_thread` is the test for it, and it was
written after the misdiagnosis precisely because a timer cannot be the witness: the reader is left
waiting for a second chunk, the chunk is delivered by a real OS thread after 1500 ms, and the test
then times 150 ms of its own sleeping. If the read parked the thread, the test cannot even report
the failure from inside the runtime that has stopped. Mutant m4, delete `set_nonblocking`:

    test waiting_for_the_next_dump_chunk_does_not_park_the_runtime_thread ... FAILED
    150ms of sibling sleeping took 1.603373006s, which is how long the reader was told to wait
    for the tail: the wait between chunks was spent inside a blocking read on this thread rather
    than on the reactor, and everything else in this runtime was stopped for the duration

    test test_giving_up_on_the_dump_releases_the_pipe ... FAILED
    assertion `left == right` failed: the shell should have killed itself before writing its dump,
    got exit=None signal=Some("timeout")

    test result: FAILED. 4 passed; 2 failed; ... finished in 61.59s

The second failure is the same defect seen from the actor: with the thread parked, the command is
not reported as killed by its own `kill -9 $$` but by its deadline.

`O_NONBLOCK` is set on the read end only, deliberately. The two ends of a pipe are separate open
file descriptions on Linux, so the write end the shell inherited stays blocking, and it has to:
the dump is about 454 KB and the buffer is 64 KiB, so the writer depends on `write` waiting for the
reader to drain rather than failing short. That was measured rather than assumed, and the first
measurement of it was false: the probe used `F_GETFL = 2`, which on Linux x86-64 is `F_SETFD`, so
it set nothing and reported both ends unchanged. With the real constants
(`{SCRATCH}/probe-pipe-ends.py`, `{SCRATCH}/pipe-ends.log`):

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/probe-pipe-ends.py
    pipe buffer capacity: 65536 bytes
    read  end before: O_RDONLY (0o0)
    write end before: O_WRONLY (0o1)
    read  end after:  O_RDONLY|O_NONBLOCK (0o4000)
    write end after:  O_WRONLY (0o1)
    a 1 MiB write into the full pipe is still inside the kernel after 2.0071s: the write end never
    saw O_NONBLOCK

== 7. Giving up on the wait did not give up on the read

With the reader cancellable, `collect_shell_state_dumps` had to be made to cancel it. A
`JoinHandle` awaiting the read is now bounded by the grace and abandoned properly:

    match tokio::time::timeout(shell_state::DUMP_COLLECT_TIMEOUT, &mut handle).await {
        ...
        Err(_elapsed) => {
            handle.abort();
            tracing::warn!("shell state dump did not arrive within {}s of the command exiting, ...");
        }
    }

`abort()` alone is not the close: it marks the task cancelled and the runtime releases the future,
and with it the `AsyncFd`, the next time it polls that task. The actor does get there, because it
returns to a select with a 10 Hz tick, but that is a scheduling fact rather than a property of the
call, and the test for it had to be written so that it says the same thing either way.

`test_giving_up_on_the_dump_releases_the_pipe` makes the holder's death the observation. The
command backgrounds a loop that writes into fd 4 once a second for a minute and then kills itself.
While our read end is open those writes succeed and the holder lives; the moment it is closed the
next write raises SIGPIPE and the holder dies. Mutant m3, drop the `handle.abort()`:

    test test_giving_up_on_the_dump_releases_the_pipe ... FAILED
    the holder was still writing into the dump pipe 20.037295047s after the command exited: the
    reader was left on it instead of being given up on with the wait, so this command still had
    someone holding its pipe open (load average at start: 1.15 ...)

    test result: FAILED. 5 passed; 1 failed; ... finished in 28.42s

Mutant m2 removes the bound itself and shows what the grace is between the command and the reply
(`{SCRATCH}/mut-m2-no-collect-bound.log`):

    test test_wedged_dump_pipe_does_not_stall_the_reply ... FAILED
    the reply only arrived once the backgrounded command had released the dump pipe (elapsed
    121.441363982s, load average at start: 1.12 3.14 4.22 ...) so nothing bounded the wait for a
    dump that never arrives; every bound here is a timer in this process, ...

    test result: FAILED. 4 passed; 2 failed; ... finished in 121.44s

== 8. The test for that close was itself wrong, twice

The first version of the holder test asked the holder to report its own death:

    ( ... ) & holder=$!; ... ; wait $holder 2>/dev/null || { echo closed > marker; }

It never wrote the marker. A process killed by SIGPIPE does not run a trap or an `||` arm; the
signal arrives in the middle of the blocked `write` and the shell is gone before anything of that
shape can execute. So the observation had to be made from outside, which is what section 7
describes.

The unit test on the reader side then failed in the full suite twice out of five rounds, with the
write accepted rather than refused:

    assertion `left == right` failed: our end of the pipe should be closed once the read is given
    up on (still accepting writes after 100 yields)
      left: None
     right: Some(EPIPE)

Waiting by yielding was the wrong tolerance. `abort()` puts the cancelled task back in the run
queue, but `yield_now` hands the current-thread runtime straight back to the task that yielded, so
a hundred yields are not a hundred looks at that queue; a 1 ms timer park does go through the
scheduler, which is also what the actor is doing while it waits on its tick. The wait is now on
timers, bounded at fifty of them, and eight consecutive full-suite rounds after that change were
green (section 9).

The failure message also gained a census of the pipe, because "a read end is still open" means two
different things depending on whether the holder is this process or a stranger:

    inode=… holders=["3691751:12(r)", "3691751:13(w)"]

It is read from `/proc/<pid>/fd` symlinks matched against the pipe's inode, with the direction
taken from the `flags:` line of `/proc/<pid>/fdinfo/<n>`, since the symlink itself does not say
which end it is. It has not fired since the tolerance changed, which is the honest state of that
diagnostic: written for a red that is now explained, kept because the next red in this family is
not.

== 9. A host nobody had looked at, and the numbers it had contaminated

Two actor tests failed deterministically in five consecutive full-suite rounds, each round
`finished in 124.6s`, each with the reply arriving exactly when the holder let go of the pipe:

    test test_wedged_dump_pipe_does_not_stall_the_reply ... FAILED
        the reply only arrived once the backgrounded command had released the dump pipe (elapsed 121.5s, ...)
    test test_giving_up_on_the_dump_releases_the_pipe ... FAILED
        the holder should still be running when the reply comes back

The same two tests passed in isolation in 8.89 s. The machine was the difference:

    $ uptime
    06:26:25 up 10 days, 8:48, 2 users, load average: 59.06, 60.43, 58.26

with 24 orphaned `/usr/bin/zsh -c 'while :; do :; done'` processes (22 h 48 m elapsed, about 94 %
CPU each) and 28 orphaned
`/bin/sh -c 'for i in $(seq 1 $(($(nproc)/2))); do (while :; do :; done) & done; wait'`
(1 h 04 m, about 72 % each), all of them `PPID=1`, left behind by aborted diagnostic drivers of
this investigation. On 28 cores that is a 2x oversubscription that never lets up, and every bound
in these tests is a timer that needs this process to be scheduled. Clearing them is
`{SCRATCH}/kill-orphans.py`; killing by process group was tried first and killed the shell running
the investigation, because those orphans have PGID 0.

Afterwards the same module filter, no source changes:

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib -- computer::local::terminal::tests
    test result: ok. 74 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 3141 filtered out; finished in 12.35s

which had been `72 passed; 2 failed; finished in 124.28s`. Shell startup, the number the timeout
budget of `docs/verification/terminal-timeout-budget-2026-10-04.log` is built on, was contaminated
in the same way and is corrected there: 0.88 / 0.83 / 0.88 s on an idle host (load average 0.25)
against the 1.32 / 1.25 / 1.45 s recorded while those loops were running.

Both failure messages now carry the load average they were measured at, e.g.
`load average at start: 1.12 3.14 4.22`, because the alternative is a red that reads as a defect.
They do not skip on load: a bound that a loaded machine cannot meet is worth knowing about, and a
silent skip is how this family of tests would have stayed broken.

One instrumented run through all of this deserves its own warning, because it produced seven
failures that did not exist. The driver that added traces to the reader inserted its chunk counter
as a second match arm with the same pattern instead of inside the existing one:

    Ok(Ok(n)) => probe(&format!("reader chunk n={n} buf={}", buf.len() + n)),
    Ok(Ok(n)) => buf.push_str(&String::from_utf8_lossy(&chunk[..n])),

The first arm wins, so the reader counted bytes and stored none. That round reported
`67 passed; 7 failed` with `cwd should persist across commands, got: /tmp` across the persistent
shell family, and the probe's own lines agreed with the lie (`collect got-dump id=… len=0`), while
also being ten times faster than the baseline. The tell was in the same build output:
`warning: xai-grok-tools (lib test) generated 1 warning`, which is the duplicated pattern. A probe
that changes the warning count is not measuring anything.

