== 11. What was not changed

- The dump itself is still ~450 KB per command on a shell with a heavy rc file. Bounding the wait
  fixes correctness; making the dump smaller is a separate piece of work and the numbers above are
  the measurement for whoever takes it.
- A dump that is rejected because the shell was killed still leaves the session on the state from
  before that command, which is the intended behaviour: the shell died, so its final state is not
  knowable.
- The grace is still 5 seconds and is still a wall-clock timer in this process. Section 9 is the
  measurement of what that means on a saturated host: the reply arrives late by however long the
  process was starved, not never. Making it a deadline that survives starvation would mean moving
  the wait off the runtime that is being starved.
- The census in section 8 is diagnostics on a failure path, not a check. The `/proc` walk is
  Linux-only; elsewhere that message says the census needs `/proc`.

== 12. 2026-10-04, later: the fix added an unsafe site, and the census said so

The sweep this change went through had a second red in it, unrelated to timing. The panic-site census
reported growth for the crate:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --check-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
    xai-grok-tools: production sites grew [16, 118, 8, 19] -> [16, 118, 8, 20]
    (unwrap, expect, panic!, unsafe)

    A crate gained a way to panic in code that ships. Remove it, or re-run
    `scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --write-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv`
    having decided to keep it.

The last column is unsafe sites in code outside `cfg(test)`, so the count was about `set_nonblocking`
from section 6, which read and wrote the pipe flags through two `unsafe { libc::fcntl(...) }` calls.
The net was +1 rather than +2 because the same change deleted one `unsafe { File::from_raw_fd(..) }`.
Attribution was done by reverting one file at a time against HEAD: with only `shell_state.rs`
reverted the baseline held, and `manager.rs` at HEAD still reproduced the growth, which is what put
the finding on this file rather than on the other modified one.

The census offers two answers and the cheaper one was wrong here. Re-running `--write-baseline`
records the new count and the ratchet moves up permanently, which is the intended path for unsafe that
is genuinely needed. This unsafe was not needed: `nix` is already a dependency of this crate, and the
facts that decide it were read out of the vendored source rather than assumed:

    $ grep -n "pub mod fcntl" -B 3 ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/nix-0.30.1/src/lib.rs
    pub mod fcntl;

    $ grep -n "pub fn fcntl<Fd" ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/nix-0.30.1/src/fcntl.rs
    pub fn fcntl<Fd: std::os::fd::AsFd>(fd: Fd, arg: FcntlArg) -> Result<c_int> {

    $ grep -n "pub type Error" ~/.cargo/registry/src/*/nix-0.30.1/src/lib.rs
    pub type Error = Errno;

The module is not behind a cargo feature, so no `Cargo.toml` line moves; the function is safe and
takes `AsFd`, which `&OwnedFd` already is; and `Error` is an alias for `Errno`, which has
`impl From<Errno> for io::Error`, so `?` lands in the `std::io::Result` the caller already returns.
The two unsafe blocks become three lines:

    fn set_nonblocking(fd: &OwnedFd) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        use nix::fcntl::{FcntlArg, OFlag, fcntl};

        // Reading the flags back first keeps any status flag we are not changing. Truncating unknown
        // bits is safe on the way in: `F_SETFL` only applies the status flags fcntl(2) lists, and
        // `OFlag` models every one of them, so a bit we dropped was never going to be applied.
        let flags = OFlag::from_bits_truncate(fcntl(fd, FcntlArg::F_GETFL)?);
        fcntl(fd, FcntlArg::F_SETFL(flags | OFlag::O_NONBLOCK))?;
        Ok(())
    }

Two things in that shape are load-bearing rather than stylistic. The read-modify-write stays, because
`F_SETFL` writes the whole status-flag set and passing only `O_NONBLOCK` would clear anything else
already on the descriptor. And truncating is safe only because `fcntl(2)` ignores the access mode and
every listed status flag is modelled by `OFlag`; that is why the comment says which of the two is the
reason, since the ordinary version of this code (`flags | O_NONBLOCK` on the raw integer) does not
have a step where bits can be lost.

`set_cloexec`, twelve lines above, still uses `libc::fcntl` directly and its two sites stay in the
record. Converting it would lower the number again, but it is not part of this change and it does not
have this section's evidence behind it.

The rewrite touches the path every command in a persistent shell takes, so the behaviour claim is the
same suite, not a type check:

    $ cargo fmt --check -p xai-grok-tools
    (rc=0)

    $ cargo clippy -p xai-grok-tools --lib --tests
    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 23.88s

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib -- computer::local::shell_state \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_persistent_shell_state_survives_a_slow_command \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_wedged_dump_pipe \
          computer::local::terminal::tests::test_giving_up_on_the_dump
    test result: ok. 31 passed; 0 failed; 1 ignored; 0 measured; 3183 filtered out; finished in 9.25s

    $ cargo test -p xai-grok-tools --lib
    test result: ok. 3212 passed; 0 failed; 3 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 30.06s

Clippy found one thing on the way. It was in the feeder thread of
`waiting_for_the_next_dump_chunk_does_not_park_the_runtime_thread`, not in the shipped function:

    warning: calling `as_bytes` after slicing a string
       --> crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/computer/local/shell_state.rs:1050:50

    tail_for_thread[written..].as_bytes()      before
    &tail_for_thread.as_bytes()[written..]     after

`written` is whatever the pipe accepted, so a short write ending inside a multi-byte character would
panic on the string slice before the assertion it was feeding could report anything. The default lint
caught an arithmetic bug in test code, which is the cheapest kind of bug this repository ever finds.

The number then went into the baseline at its new value, which makes the ratchet one site tighter
than it was before this change rather than one looser:

    $ grep -n "^xai-grok-tools\b" scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
    70:xai-grok-tools    16    118    8    18

    $ python3 scripts/ci/panic-site-census.py --check-baseline scripts/ci/panic-site-baseline.tsv
    baseline holds: 97 crates, 0 fewer production sites than recorded
    (rc=0)

and it still bites. With that row reading 17 instead of 18, in a copy under `{SCRATCH}`, the same
command reports:

    xai-grok-tools: production sites grew [16, 118, 8, 17] -> [16, 118, 8, 18]
    (unwrap, expect, panic!, unsafe)
    (rc=1)

The full sweep after both fixes: `all gates passed on the host (28 run, 4 skipped)`, including
`PASS  panic-site census` and `PASS  platform-gated tests`. The second of those is the ledger rewrite
recorded in section 12 of `docs/verification/platform-gated-tests-2026-10-03.log`, which the same
sweep had also turned red.
